<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the MAC APP STORE build, and only for it. The Developer ID
  build (scripts/build-desktop.sh) ships with none of this: direct distribution
  asks for a hardened runtime, the App Store asks for a sandbox, and the two
  are different promises.

  The sandbox is the whole reason this file exists. Inside it the app may only
  do what is listed here, and what this app does is:

    * talk to the API over HTTPS and keep a socket open to it, and let
      WKWebView stream VK's HLS — all of it OUTGOING, so the network client
      entitlement covers every one of them;
    * nothing else. No file access beyond its own container, no microphone, no
      camera, no printing. The desktop never logs in to VK (it is paired from
      a phone) and never opens a file the user chose.

  The identifier below is the PHONE app's, on purpose. A macOS app that shares
  the iOS bundle id joins the same App Store Connect record as another platform
  instead of needing a second listing with its own name, description and
  screenshots. The Developer ID build keeps com.envarg.visky.desktop and is
  untouched by any of this — scripts/build-desktop-mas.sh rewrites the
  identifier in the copy it signs, never in shell/tauri.conf.json.

  It has to match the App ID in the developer portal and the one inside the
  provisioning profile, or codesign rejects the bundle with a mismatch that
  names none of the three.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
	<string>N853W9Q344.com.envarg.visky</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
	<string>N853W9Q344</string>
</dict>
</plist>
